    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. that makes sense. i'm mostly worried about some weird edge cases though—like when users with limited vision try to navigate through complex data tables or when certain UI elements just don't seem to register correctly with the screen reader during testing. what should i look for to catch those specific types of issues?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When dealing with complex data tables, you need to ensure proper semantic HTML is used, like using `<th>` tags correctly for headers and `scope` attributes to define column/row relationships. If a screen reader is skipping over elements, it often means the focus order is illogical or the element lacks a proper accessible name. You should test by navigating *only* with the keyboard (Tab key) to catch focus order issues, which is vital for low-vision users.",
      "source": "target",
